  malformed JSON, and no throw when [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m is unavailable. [38;5;153mlogin()[39m is harder — [38;5;153mapp.js[39m calls
  [38;5;153mdocument.getElementById("login-form")[39m at load time, so requiring it in Node blows up before any test runs. The cheap
  fix is to guard the wiring ([38;5;153mif (typeof document !== "undefined")[39m) so the file can be imported headlessly; that's a
  two-line change to code we're already touching, and without it [38;5;153mlogin()[39m can only be checked by hand in a browser.

  [1mMy recommendation: unit tests only, using Node's built-in runner[0m ([38;5;153mnode --test[39m, plus a [38;5;153m"test"[39m script). It needs zero
  dependencies, which keeps the repo's dependency-free posture intact. Two small accommodations: [38;5;153msession.js[39m gets a
  trailing [38;5;153mif (typeof module !== "undefined") module.exports = Session;[39m so it works as both a browser global and a
  CommonJS import — matching [38;5;153msrc/[39m's existing convention — and the tests inject a fake [38;5;153msessionStorage[39m object rather than
  needing a DOM.

  [1mWhat I'd argue against, for now:[0m end-to-end infrastructure (a browser driver is a heavy dependency for one form with a
  stubbed login that makes no network call) and fuzz/mutation testing (there's no real input-processing surface — the
  only parse is one [38;5;153mJSON.parse[39m already guarded by [38;5;153mtry/catch[39m). Linting and formatting are genuinely cheap and I'd take
  them if you want them, but with four small files and no team, the payoff is thin. Happy to add any of these if you
  disagree — you know the trajectory of this repo better than I do.

  This is the last section. Once you've answered, I'll write the spec to
  [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-login-userid-session-design.md[39m, run it through the review gates, and hand it to you
  to read before anything gets built.

